WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.212
Netflix says it doesn't let brands

00:00:03.212 --> 00:00:05.180
pay for product placement.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.450
So how do you explain this?

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.650
JJ: You guys see that?

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.670
That's the Malibu 24 MXZ, the
world's finest Wakesetter.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.440
No. 1 in luxury, quality, and performance.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.417
Narrator: Or this?

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:18.728
Antoni: Stores like this,

00:00:18.728 --> 00:00:23.010
they're designed for the
Bobby Camps of the world, OK?

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.620
It's all organized in a very
logical, structured way.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.487
Narrator: Or this?

00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:31.243
Steve: It's finger-lickin' good.

00:00:32.512 --> 00:00:35.740
Narrator: All of those scenes
are from Netflix originals,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.112
and they all feature branded products

00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:41.000
in a way that feels a
lot like advertising.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.250
So if Netflix says these
aren't paid promotions,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.520
how are all these brands winding up

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.757
in your favorite Netflix shows?

00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:50.748
Clip: Refreshment you can trust.

00:00:50.748 --> 00:00:53.290
A bottle of ice-cold Coca-Cola

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.693
when you're thirsty.

00:00:54.693 --> 00:00:56.109
Lucas: Sweeter.

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:57.984
Bolder.

00:00:57.984 --> 00:00:59.425
Better.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:01.930
Narrator: Product
placement is nothing new,

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.331
and in the streaming era,

00:01:03.331 --> 00:01:06.177
the practice can take a
lot of different forms.

00:01:06.177 --> 00:01:09.040
Lenny: Because first, I have
to drink my Cherry Coke Zero.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.750
Narrator: Some Netflix competitors,

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.816
like Hulu, have openly pursued

00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:15.291
a variety of product-placement deals.

00:01:15.291 --> 00:01:16.667
Amazon has ventured into

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.450
shoppable product
placements for Prime Video,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.724
and Disney Plus is reportedly open

00:01:21.724 --> 00:01:23.610
to securing product-placement deals

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.900
for its upcoming original shows.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.069
So what about the world's
biggest streaming service?

00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:30.956
Whenever Netflix has commented

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:33.623
on the brand presence
in its original series,

00:01:33.623 --> 00:01:34.764
the company has said

00:01:34.764 --> 00:01:37.400
that it generally doesn't
take cash from brands

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.510
in exchange for showing
their products on screen.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.518
That's the old pay-to-play
model of product placement,

00:01:43.518 --> 00:01:45.951
where brands simply buy screen time.

00:01:45.951 --> 00:01:47.370
And in today's industry,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.820
it's just one of many ways for brands

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.280
to land their products in a show or movie.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.286
Most of Netflix's statements, though,

00:01:54.286 --> 00:01:56.640
refer to only this narrow definition

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.481
of paid product placement

00:01:58.481 --> 00:01:59.739
and don't really address

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:01.690
whether the company is working with brands

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:05.060
in other ways behind the
scenes of Netflix productions.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:06.885
That may be because Netflix

00:02:06.885 --> 00:02:09.440
wants to protect its ad-free image.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.579
Dominic Artzrouni: I
think it's because Netflix

00:02:10.579 --> 00:02:13.629
really wants to avoid that label of them

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:15.280
selling out in any way.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.253
Narrator: This is Dominic Artzrouni,

00:02:17.253 --> 00:02:19.336
and he's the founder of Concave,

00:02:19.336 --> 00:02:20.694
a market-research firm

00:02:20.694 --> 00:02:23.420
that tracks brands'
placements in entertainment.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.280
Artzrouni: Product placement,
in most people's mind,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.232
is highly associated
with paid advertising,

00:02:28.232 --> 00:02:30.014
being invasive, selling out.

00:02:30.014 --> 00:02:31.600
Narrator: Despite Netflix's efforts

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.833
to resist the product-placement label,

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:36.049
one 2018 study found

00:02:36.049 --> 00:02:38.970
that Netflix originals
have more product placement

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.713
on average than non-Netflix originals.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.855
And Netflix has a lot to gain

00:02:44.855 --> 00:02:47.113
from showcasing brands in its content,

00:02:47.113 --> 00:02:48.580
whether or not those brands

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.100
actually pay for the opportunity.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.383
Take the wildly popular
Netflix original movie

00:02:54.383 --> 00:02:57.330
2018's "To All the Boys
I've Loved Before."

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.710
Chris: We can't have these
contraband Subway cups

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:01.280
out in the open.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.470
PS, that sub is tight, right?

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.150
Narrator: There's no
mention of Subway sandwiches

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.220
in the novel this movie's based on.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.370
But Netflix's rom-com made
Subway into a defining feature

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.840
of Lara Jean's best friend, Chris,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.334
who loves the chain's sandwiches so much

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:20.590
that she texts in Subway-branded emojis.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:23.440
Subway is known to push
for a speaking role

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.600
for its products in TV shows.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.497
Kamekona: But this
Subway sandwich? So ono.

00:03:28.497 --> 00:03:29.830
Steve: How many of these did you order?

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:30.903
Kamekona: Five.

00:03:30.903 --> 00:03:32.294
Kono: Five foot-longs?

00:03:32.294 --> 00:03:33.460
Kamekona: It was a good deal.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.690
Narrator: And Netflix
clearly granted it that

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.210
in "To All the Boys."

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.270
Business Insider reported that a third

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.910
of Chris' screen time involved Subway

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.278
in some way, and the brand was weaved

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:46.600
into the very plotline of the movie.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.180
At one point, Chris is
craving Subway so badly

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.998
that she ditches their usual lunch

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:53.243
in the cafeteria,

00:03:53.243 --> 00:03:56.050
leaving Lara Jean to eat with her crush.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.345
Netflix got something in return for this

00:03:58.345 --> 00:04:01.713
when the "To All the Boys"
sequel rolled around in 2020.

00:04:01.713 --> 00:04:04.660
The movie again featured
aggressive Subway marketing

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.330
in its prop design and dialogue.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:08.463
Lara Jean: Why are we in woodshop for our

00:04:08.463 --> 00:04:09.847
Valentine Subway tradition?

00:04:09.847 --> 00:04:11.478
Narrator: And Subway in turn

00:04:11.478 --> 00:04:14.194
launched a special campaign
to promote the film

00:04:14.194 --> 00:04:15.980
through its own marketing channels.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.240
Given that millions of
people eat Subway every day,

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:22.010
that's some valuable
publicity for Netflix.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:25.125
This type of deal is called
co-promotional marketing,

00:04:25.125 --> 00:04:26.270
and it basically means

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.750
that the two companies trade promotions.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.150
Netflix gave Subway cultural relevance

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.433
in "To All the Boys,"

00:04:32.433 --> 00:04:35.453
and Subway helped Netflix
attract more subscribers

00:04:35.453 --> 00:04:37.840
who want to watch this movie.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.620
Clearly, it's a smart
business move for Netflix

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.861
to embed brands in its programming,

00:04:42.861 --> 00:04:44.886
even in cases where the product placement

00:04:44.886 --> 00:04:46.620
seems to happen organically,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.560
like it did with Eggos
in "Stranger Things."

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.242
Eggo said it was as surprised

00:04:51.242 --> 00:04:53.990
as anyone else by its waffles' big role

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:55.420
in the show's first season,

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.470
but after seeing an
unexpected boost in sales,

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.930
the brand decided to partner with Netflix

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.339
and play up its "Stranger Things" moment.

00:05:09.888 --> 00:05:11.560
The two companies inked a deal

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.718
for a series of cross promotions,

00:05:13.718 --> 00:05:16.020
including a joint Super Bowl ad spot

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.140
that used a throwback Eggo commercial

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.660
to tease the second season

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.060
of "Stranger Things."

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.390
Eggo, meanwhile, continued to appear

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:26.260
throughout seasons two
and three of the show,

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.930
a win-win for both companies.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:32.210
Plus, after featuring a
brand in one of its shows,

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.240
Netflix has a pretext to
team up with that company

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.030
on all kinds of product tie-ins.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.337
Judging from the long
line of product launches

00:05:40.337 --> 00:05:41.830
around "Stranger Things,"

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:43.720
Netflix is fully leaning into

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:45.845
this kind of merchandising now.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.836
And the show's been a perfect vehicle

00:05:47.836 --> 00:05:48.870
for this strategy,

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.611
thanks to all the opportunities

00:05:50.611 --> 00:05:52.952
for '80s nostalgia marketing.

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.660
Netflix has linked up with brands

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.900
to make "Stranger Things" waffle boxes,

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.685
several models of bikes,

00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:01.870
and a whole slate of
clothing and sneaker lines.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.310
Characters even promote
some of the product lines

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.727
in their costumes, with Dustin, Eleven,

00:06:06.727 --> 00:06:09.118
and Jim Hopper wearing pieces

00:06:09.118 --> 00:06:10.984
from Netflix's clothing collabs

00:06:10.984 --> 00:06:13.618
in several pivotal
scenes from season three.

00:06:13.618 --> 00:06:15.646
Calvin: That's a lot of
color for you, chief.

00:06:15.646 --> 00:06:17.438
Hopper: Its cutting-edge stuff. All right?

00:06:17.438 --> 00:06:19.290
Narrator: Iconic characters on the show

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.280
become inextricably linked
to certain products,

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.512
just like we saw with Chris and Subway

00:06:24.512 --> 00:06:26.600
in "To All the Boys I've Loved Before."

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.083
In that way, Netflix seems to be following

00:06:29.083 --> 00:06:32.003
the example of franchises like James Bond,

00:06:32.003 --> 00:06:35.571
a case study of great,
character-based product placement.

00:06:35.571 --> 00:06:37.258
Artzrouni: The Eggo waffles become like

00:06:37.258 --> 00:06:39.948
a staple of Eleven, and
no one sees any of that

00:06:39.948 --> 00:06:41.285
as forced product placement

00:06:41.285 --> 00:06:43.360
because it comes intertwined
with the character.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.620
The perfect example of this is James Bond.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.545
Aston Martin has been with James Bond

00:06:47.545 --> 00:06:48.511
since the beginning.

00:06:48.511 --> 00:06:51.491
Narrator: In 2019,
Dominic's firm, Concave,

00:06:51.491 --> 00:06:52.915
released a report finding

00:06:52.915 --> 00:06:56.150
that over 100 brands
appeared in the third season

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:57.754
of "Stranger Things" in total,

00:06:57.754 --> 00:06:59.520
giving those brands an estimated

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.303
$15 million in ad value

00:07:02.303 --> 00:07:05.280
within just three days
after the season's release.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.308
As of June 2020, Concave estimated

00:07:08.308 --> 00:07:12.070
that number has gone up to $55 million.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:14.620
And the biggest winner was Coca-Cola.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:16.911
Concave found that the company's drinks,

00:07:16.911 --> 00:07:18.795
vending machines, and signage

00:07:18.795 --> 00:07:22.270
helped make it the most
visible brand in season three.

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.510
Its presence peaked in the finale,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.560
when Lucas interrupts all the action

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.160
to give a big, long speech
on the merits of New Coke,

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.770
the flavor that launched
and flopped back in 1985.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.440
Lucas: It's like Carpenter's "The Thing."

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.360
The original is the classic,
no question about it.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.760
But the remake?

00:07:43.082 --> 00:07:45.081
[slurps]

00:07:45.081 --> 00:07:46.855
[sighs]

00:07:46.855 --> 00:07:48.276
[smacks lips]

00:07:48.276 --> 00:07:49.717
Sweeter.

00:07:49.717 --> 00:07:51.550
Bolder.

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:52.383
Better.

00:07:52.383 --> 00:07:55.080
Narrator: This moment felt
a lot like a commercial.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.900
Clip: You mean you don't
drink Coca-Cola Classic?

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:58.500
Us kids like Coke.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.033
It's light, smooth. It's the latest.

00:08:01.033 --> 00:08:03.117
Narrator: And in a way, it was.

00:08:03.117 --> 00:08:06.600
Right on time, Coca-Cola
brought back New Coke

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.460
as a special re-release with Netflix.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:12.730
Scenes like this make the
brand presence on Netflix

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:14.820
all but impossible to ignore.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.270
Yet, when Netflix saw Concave's report

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.403
on all the brands in season three,

00:08:19.403 --> 00:08:21.710
Netflix's team reached out to Dominic

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:24.480
to push back on the use of one term.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:25.750
Artzrouni: They hated the use

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:27.393
of the term "product placement."

00:08:27.393 --> 00:08:30.140
Narrator: Netflix said that
it didn't accept payment

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:32.970
for any of the placements
in "Stranger Things."

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.637
Artzrouni: They claim
that product placement,

00:08:35.637 --> 00:08:37.561
you should only call it that

00:08:37.561 --> 00:08:39.161
when money is exchanging hands.

00:08:39.161 --> 00:08:43.098
And that's a total misunderstanding
of product placement.

00:08:43.098 --> 00:08:45.514
Narrator: It's true that
co-promotional deals,

00:08:45.514 --> 00:08:47.797
like those with Coca-Cola or Eggo,

00:08:47.797 --> 00:08:49.887
don't require an exchange of money

00:08:49.887 --> 00:08:52.370
between Netflix and
the brands it features.

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.280
But as we saw in the Coke scene,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.972
just because a brand
appearance wasn't paid for

00:08:56.972 --> 00:08:58.341
doesn't mean it'll be

00:08:58.341 --> 00:09:00.265
any lighter on the commercialism.

00:09:00.265 --> 00:09:02.220
Artzrouni: You can argue
over who's doing the placing.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.960
Is it the company, or is it the producer?

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:05.932
But at the end of the day,
we make no distinction,

00:09:05.932 --> 00:09:07.212
because the viewer doesn't know.

00:09:07.212 --> 00:09:09.984
It doesn't really matter
how the brand got there.

00:09:09.984 --> 00:09:11.820
Narrator: Scripted brand cameos,

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.907
like all the Subway references
in "To All the Boys,"

00:09:14.907 --> 00:09:16.110
might make you wonder:

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:17.637
How did the brand get there?

00:09:17.637 --> 00:09:20.370
And how much of a say do
they get in the writing?

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.730
To answer this question,

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:24.946
we went to Branded
Entertainment Network, or BEN,

00:09:24.946 --> 00:09:26.562
a company that specializes

00:09:26.562 --> 00:09:28.851
in setting up product-placement deals

00:09:28.851 --> 00:09:29.976
and that's gotten brands

00:09:29.976 --> 00:09:32.440
into a bunch of Netflix originals.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:33.898
Erin Schmidt: So, we take what the brand

00:09:33.898 --> 00:09:35.206
is trying to accomplish,

00:09:35.206 --> 00:09:37.360
and we go and talk to content creators.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.192
In some cases, we get to read the script.

00:09:39.192 --> 00:09:41.930
And if those content creators
are interested in that brand,

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.030
believe in what the brand
is trying to accomplish

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:45.700
within their piece of content,

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.680
we can continue that conversation.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.430
The producer or the content creator

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:51.750
really guides the creative from there.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:55.840
It's very easily not
going to work for a brand

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.570
if it just doesn't fit into
the storyline organically.

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:01.080
Narrator: So, if producers, not brands,

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.371
are running the show,

00:10:02.371 --> 00:10:04.830
why do we still get moments like this?

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.612
Pope: 2020 mode with 24 MXZ.
World's finest Wakesetter.

00:10:08.612 --> 00:10:11.774
No. 1 in quality, luxury, and performance.

00:10:11.774 --> 00:10:13.700
Narrator: Well, the producers themselves

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:17.020
might be getting money or
free stuff from brands.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.710
And since many of the
biggest Netflix originals

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.030
aren't actually produced at Netflix,

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:24.590
the streaming company can
keep some careful distance

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:27.550
from all of this, as it'll
be the production studio,

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:29.330
not Netflix itself,

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.360
interfacing with brands
and agencies like BEN.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.854
In fact, a lot of the Netflix shows

00:10:34.854 --> 00:10:36.595
that BEN has placed products in

00:10:36.595 --> 00:10:38.904
have been produced outside Netflix,

00:10:38.904 --> 00:10:42.164
like "Fuller House," where
they landed Jose Cuervo;

00:10:42.164 --> 00:10:45.522
"Jessica Jones," where
they got Tincup Whiskey;

00:10:45.522 --> 00:10:47.400
or "House of Cards," where they got

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.710
regular appearances for Dunkin' Donuts

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.254
and General Motors.

00:10:51.254 --> 00:10:54.345
In titles like these that
are produced out of house,

00:10:54.345 --> 00:10:56.380
Netflix will sometimes get a say

00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:59.360
in whether brands make
their way into the script.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:01.547
But if there are money
or contracts involved,

00:11:01.547 --> 00:11:03.890
Netflix can pretty much stay out of it,

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.598
while quietly letting
their producers take fees

00:11:06.598 --> 00:11:09.890
or free products and services from brands.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:13.080
That happened with the
2018 movie "Like Father,"

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:16.200
another Netflix original
produced out of house.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.492
Royal Caribbean gave the film crew

00:11:18.492 --> 00:11:20.716
free use of one of its cruise liners.

00:11:20.716 --> 00:11:23.270
In other words, a free filming location

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:25.238
for most of the scenes in the movie.

00:11:25.238 --> 00:11:27.060
The producers modified the script

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.660
to include details specific
to the cruise liner

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.550
and the activities offered on board,

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:33.698
even adding a whole game-show scene

00:11:33.698 --> 00:11:35.499
that wasn't in the script before.

00:11:35.499 --> 00:11:37.560
This kind of assistance from brands

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.235
in return for product placement

00:11:39.235 --> 00:11:43.010
can help producers save
big and stay under budget.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:44.730
And it's good for Netflix too.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.929
Schmidt: For every platform,

00:11:46.929 --> 00:11:49.362
you want to empower your producers.

00:11:49.362 --> 00:11:52.010
Allowing a producer to strike deals

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:55.160
or to do trade out with
expensive opportunities,

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:58.680
whether it be a location or an
auto or all of those things,

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.570
you're empowering that producer
to do that extra shoot,

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:03.620
to do that really cool chase scene

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.026
or whatever it might be

00:12:05.026 --> 00:12:07.915
to move that piece of
content to the next level.

00:12:07.915 --> 00:12:09.070
Narrator: On the one hand,

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.240
these types of
behind-the-scenes arrangements

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:13.206
can result in pretty heavy-handed

00:12:13.206 --> 00:12:15.066
brand messaging on screen.

00:12:15.066 --> 00:12:16.491
But on the flip side,

00:12:16.491 --> 00:12:18.824
it can sometimes feel more intrusive

00:12:18.824 --> 00:12:21.741
when shows avoid product
placement altogether.

00:12:21.741 --> 00:12:24.110
Artzrouni: The thing I find
actually more unnerving

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.618
than product placement
is when you see shows

00:12:26.618 --> 00:12:28.370
where they've removed the badges off cars,

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:30.120
or they've taped up the back of computers,

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.424
or, you know, if you have,
like, blank cans of soda.

00:12:32.424 --> 00:12:34.708
That's not realistic.

00:12:34.708 --> 00:12:36.705
Narrator: Netflix's use
of product placement

00:12:36.705 --> 00:12:38.780
is a trade-off for viewers.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.788
The kind of deep brand integration

00:12:40.788 --> 00:12:42.371
you see in Netflix originals

00:12:42.371 --> 00:12:44.740
is less disruptive than commercials,

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:47.548
and it might help Netflix stay ad-free

00:12:47.548 --> 00:12:50.875
while continuing to churn
out high-quality content.

00:12:50.875 --> 00:12:54.000
But when brands become
stars in their own right,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:55.820
it sometimes blurs the line

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:58.733
between what's an ad and what isn't.

